abstract={The cage fish farming is a potential source of fish production in many parts of the world, which has abundant water resources and has been an excellent source of livelihood for the poor and low-income groups. However, cage aquaculture is considered a source of increased eutrophication and nutrients in aquatic ecosystems due to fish feeding. The enclosed ecosystem of the Caspian Sea has been affected by many types of pollution over the past 30 years and is highly stressed. Pollution caused by human activities such as heavy metals, agricultural pesticides, increase in nutrient load due to fertilization of agricultural fields are important and threatening factors of the unique aquatic resources of the Caspian Sea. The purpose of this paper is to investigate changes in the structure of the environment and nutrients around the marine fish farming cage located in the Caspian region, south of the Caspian Sea. This study was conducted in 3 stations near the fish farming site in cages and 3 reference stations in 5 times during 1397 and 1398. In this study, the total abundance of comb (Mnemiopsis leidyi) in the stations adjacent to the fish farming site (13200 ind.m-3) compared to stations far from the fish farming site (9500 ind.m-3) has increased by 42% in 2018-2020. The length group of M. leidyi was less than 5 mm and the predominant population was recored in the adjacent stations and away from the cage fish farming site. In this study, for the first time in February 2020 a new non-native species of Beroe ovata were observed in the cages adjacent to fish farms. The frequency of this comb fluctuated between 27 and 47 ind.m-3 in stations adjacent to the cages and B. ovata was not observed at the stations far from the fish farming site. Total nitrogen concentrations fluctuated between 0.7 and 1.4 mg.l-1 at the station far from the cage fish farming site and adjacent to the cage, respectively. Also, the highest concentration of total phosphorus was observed at the station adjacent to the cage with more than 0.2 mg.l-1 and the lowest level of 0.03 mg.l-1 was observed at the station away from the cage. Sea water turbidity in near the fish farming cage was recorded 4 times higher than stations far from the cage. In this study, 42 phytoplankton species including diatoms (25 species), chlorophyta (3 species), cyanophyta (7 species), dinoflagella (6 species) and Euglenaphyta (1 species) were identified. The diatom with more than 80% of the predominant abundance of phytoplankton structure in most of the months under study. The results of this study showed that the abundance of phytoplankton and non-native species of Pseudo-nitzschia seriata from the diatoms were higher in the stations adjacent in the cage fish farming site than in the far from cage stations. The CCA analysis also showed a correlation between the abundance of phytoplankton taxa with non-biological factors. In this study, 10 groups of zooplankton were identified; the predominant composition of zooplankton was Acartia tonsa with the highest abundance. Statistical t-test did not show a significant difference between the abundance of zooplankton in adjacent cage fish farming stations and reference stations. The results showed that the highest amount of organic matter in the adjacent fish farming stations in the sea cage, between 96 and 80% and the lowest amount of organic matter in the far stations ranged from 16 to 46%. Studies on nutrients levels in the seabed showed that the highest of phosphorus was measured 495 mg/kg at the station adjacent to the fish farming site and the lowest amount of phosphorus was recorded 420 mg/kg at the reference station fish farming cage. Also, the amount of nitrogen level in the station adjacent to the fish farm site in the sea cage was 1390 mg/kg, the amount of nitrogen level in the reference station of the fish farming site in the sea cage was 740 mg/kg. Probably an increase in the amount of nutrients from feeding and excreted waste from fish farming in cages were the main reasons for the increase of M. leidyi abundance and the occurrence of a new non-native species of B. ovata, increase in phytoplankton abundance, increase in nutrients and organic nutrients sediments at stations around the cage fish farming site. Since the present study was conducted at a fish farming site in the sea cage, increasing the capacity of fish farming cages in the Caspian Sea, its destructive effects on the native species of the Caspian Sea will be greater. Therefore, environmental assessment and aquatic ecosystem production capacity should be estimated and the monitoring plan of sea cage sites in the surrounding area and adjacent to fish farming in sea cages should be done to monitor and control any environmental changes in the water source.},
